From 74fe961870f502ba04047b5afbcf80113e804ae8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:41:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CurlFile: a read returns the data it asked for without
 waiting for more

In CCurlFile::CReadState::FillBuffer(), a multi_perform() that puts in
the buffer what the read asked for (a read asks for one byte) still went
on to select() before the loop looked at its condition again: the data
waited for the next packet from the network or, when the source paused,
for the longer of 200 ms and curl's own timer (up to a second).

Measured with the read path of this file (CReadState::Read and
FillBuffer, built on the host against libcurl) and a local server that
sends 64 KiB every 1.5 s: each burst reached the reader 199 to 1000 ms
after it was sent (median 700 ms); with this change, 0 ms in all six.
A transfer that stalls ends as before (26 s, curl's low-speed limit).

The result of the perform is now looked at first: when the buffer holds
what was asked, or is full, the loop ends, as its condition would have
ended it after the wait. What is read, and when a transfer ends, do not
change.

The idea comes from primez (pannal's Kodi tree, "CurlFile: Return
buffered data before another network wait").
---
 xbmc/filesystem/CurlFile.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/xbmc/filesystem/CurlFile.cpp b/xbmc/filesystem/CurlFile.cpp
index 0dafd9d..d8d7c08 100644
--- a/xbmc/filesystem/CurlFile.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/filesystem/CurlFile.cpp
@@ -1917,6 +1917,12 @@ int8_t CCurlFile::CReadState::FillBuffer(unsigned int want)
     {
       case CURLM_OK:
       {
+        // The perform may have given what was asked, or filled the buffer: the loop ends then, and
+        // a wait for more data from the network first would only hold back what is here (until the
+        // next packet, or up to the timeout of curl when the source pauses).
+        if (m_buffer.getMaxReadSize() >= want || m_buffer.getMaxWriteSize() == 0)
+          break;
+
         int maxfd = -1;
         FD_ZERO(&fdread);
         FD_ZERO(&fdwrite);
